# Cascadia - Project Rules

Persistent project decisions. Append new rules; do not rewrite existing ones.

## Identity

- Cascadia is a third-person Soulslike. Gameplay is authoritative; animation, models, effects and sound are downstream adapters that must conform to gameplay timing, never define it.
- The foundation must behave correctly using primitive geometry and placeholder presentation.

## Movement

- Locomotion is grounded. There is NO jumping, platforming, traversal, climbing, vaulting, ledge grabbing, mantling, or aerial combat in the foundation. `jump` is a reserved input slot with no implementation.
- L3 is reserved for a possible future Jump / Advanced Mobility and is not implemented.

## Input architecture

- Gameplay reads semantic actions only, through CascadiaInput. Gameplay code must never read physical keys, mouse buttons or controller buttons directly.
- SPRINT and DODGE are separate gameplay actions with independent tuning, even when a controller presents both on Circle.
- Controller Circle is a device-level contextual source (`mobility_button`): tap = dodge, hold = sprint, resolved in the input layer. Keyboard keeps them physically separate on purpose: Left Shift = sprint, Space = dodge.
- Reserved input slots with NO implementation: `ranged_attack` (L2, future Ranged / Firearm) and `jump` (L3).

## Future controller layout (recorded, NOT implemented)

- Tap X = contextual Use / Interact.
- Hold X = open the Quick Bar.
- While the Quick Bar is held open, the D-pad selects; releasing confirms.
- Do not build the quick bar, a radial menu, or a complex inventory until its milestone.

## Architecture

- Physical actor collision and combat collision (hitboxes / hurtboxes) are separate concerns on separate physics layers. Combat volumes must never be used as physical body collision.
- Systems stay modular: input, locomotion, stamina, combat, damage, enemy AI, targeting, game state and animation presentation do not leak into each other.
- Actors own their own combat state (can act, can be damaged, invulnerable, staggered, dead, current action).
- Do not solve systemic problems with unexplained positional offsets.
- Never implement more than the current milestone; do not prematurely combine systems that are still being tuned.

## File hygiene

- Never delete, rename or move project files as part of routine development.
- When a file looks obsolete, temporary, duplicated or superseded, record it in CASCADIA_DELETION_MANIFEST.md for manual review instead.
- Do not block or stop a valid development task because cleanup is pending, and do not repeatedly ask whether files may be deleted.
- Diagnostic and test material belongs under `scripts/diagnostics/` and should be named `*_debug_*` or `*_test_*` where practical.

## Documentation discipline (automatic)

- `CASCADIA_DELETION_MANIFEST.md` and `.summer/plans/CASCADIA_MILESTONE_ROADMAP.md` are updated AUTOMATICALLY as part of the work, not afterwards and not only when asked.
- Manifest: record every new diagnostic, temporary or cleanup-candidate file; every superseded script or scene; every newly discovered candidate; every change to an existing candidate's status; and every recurring editor registration, class-cache or persistence problem.
- Roadmap: record every milestone status change, every defect found or fixed, newly confirmed behaviour, newly unverified behaviour, and every change to the deferred list.
- Record these at the moment they are discovered. A roadmap that lags the project is worse than none, because it is trusted.
- Recording a candidate is the action; deletion stays manual and is never performed autonomously.

## Movement authority (recorded 2026-09-12, end-of-session review)

- A COMMITTED action (dodge, backstep, attack, parry) is authoritative over its own movement for its whole duration. Ordinary locomotion must not overwrite, blend into, or re-orient it.
- Presentation must never become the accidental authority. Primitive movement, facing and placement are PLACEHOLDERS for a future animation adapter; once that adapter exists, the authoritative gameplay state selects the animation and primitive motion stops being the final presentation.
- Body orientation during a committed evasion belongs to the locked gameplay facing, not to current velocity. Do not derive facing from velocity while a committed action owns the body.
- Position must not be rewritten during a committed action by a system that did not author it - for example step-up resolution adding displacement the action never asked for.
- VERIFIED 2026-09-12: velocity arbitration is already correct (`_apply_horizontal()` sets dodge velocity and returns early). Orientation and position arbitration are NOT - see roadmap section 8F. Recorded as a bounded future pass; do NOT implement it as part of unrelated work.

## Input architecture - amendment (2026-09-12, Sprint/Dodge/Backstep pass)

- AMENDS the earlier "Keyboard keeps them physically separate on purpose" rule. Keyboard Left Shift is now ALSO a shared tap/hold command, resolved in the input layer exactly like the controller: hold = sprint, tap = dodge. Space REMAINS bound as a dedicated dodge, so nothing was removed.
- CascadeInput resolves TWO command sources through ONE shared `_advance_source()`: `mobility_button` (controller Circle) and `sprint` (Left Shift). Both produce the same tap/hold decision, so keyboard and controller cannot drift apart.
- A tap of the shared command with NO movement input produces a BACKSTEP, not a forward dodge. A tap WITH movement input produces a directional dodge. This selection lives in PlayerController, not in the input layer.
- Dodge direction and the dodge's kind (DIRECTIONAL / BACKSTEP) plus its facing (FORWARD / BACKWARD / LEFT / RIGHT) are GAMEPLAY state exposed by DodgeComponent for the animation adapter. Facing is classified against the CAMERA basis, not the body, so a sustained strafe cannot collapse into "forward".
- Lock-on does NOT exist and is NOT implemented. Dodge direction is camera-relative. Do not build lock-on-relative dodge direction until lock-on is its own milestone.
- Animation remains deferred: there is no animation system, so these semantic values are state for a future adapter, not clips to play.

## Save/load input bindings - HOST-OWNED KEYS (recorded 2026-09-13, M10.6)

- MEASURED, not assumed: in the Summer Engine host, **F9 is the host's PAUSE TOGGLE** (sets `Engine.time_scale = 0` and releases the mouse while `SceneTree.paused` stays false) and **F8 ENDS THE DEBUG SESSION**. Both were proven with the game's only save/load consumer disabled, so no Cascadia code could be responsible.
- Therefore `quick_load` (F9) and `quick_load_alt` (F8) can NEVER reach the game reliably. They remain in the InputMap UNUSED for reference; do not build on them.
- The load path reads `load_run` (**F7**) and `load_run_alt` (**F11**). Both were measured safe: delivered to the game, `scale` stayed 1.000, `paused=false`, mouse stayed captured, no input frame skipped. F1-F7, F10-F12 and ordinary letters are safe; F8 and F9 are not.
- `quick_save` (F5), `quick_save_alt` (F6) and `new_run` (F10) are safe and remain as they are.
- Do NOT "restore" F9 as the load key. A host-owned key produces a frozen game that every in-engine health check reports as healthy.

## Window focus and gameplay input (recorded 2026-09-13, M10.7)

- A focus change must never be able to WEDGE the game. The per-frame reconciliation in `CascadiaInput` is RESUME-ONLY: it may clear a suspension, never create one. `has_focus()` is a report, not an authority to freeze gameplay, because a host that never flips it back would suspend the layer every frame forever.
- A suspension must always be escapable by the player. A deliberate key press or mouse-button press resumes gameplay input (`_input` presence recovery, which runs before GUI handling so no Control can eat it). Motion may NOT resume anything - motion can arrive from the host with no interaction, and treating it as presence re-creates the original defect.
- `mouse_look_enabled` is gameplay's declared INTENT and is owned by Escape alone. Focus transitions must never clear it, so capture can be re-asserted on return without the player clicking twice.
- The input layer owns focus and capture; gameplay systems must not add their own focus rules. Verify any future change with `scripts/diagnostics/focus_input_routing_probe_debug.gd`, which measures the focused, focus-lost and stuck-with-no-focus-in states separately.

## Mouse capture must be taken IN AN INPUT EVENT (recorded 2026-09-13, M10.7a)

- MEASURED CONSEQUENCE: requesting pointer capture from a per-frame callback is NOT enough in the Summer Engine host. Pointer lock is a USER-GESTURE permission there, so a request made outside a real input event can be dropped while looking successful from inside the game - the camera responds and the cursor stays free, which was the user's report.
- Therefore the click itself takes the cursor back: `CascadiaInput._recover_capture_on_press()` runs from `_input` on every mouse-button press, inside that event's own call stack. The per-frame keeper remains as a backstop for the focused-and-captured case; it is not the mechanism that recovers from a drop.
- That recovering click must ALSO set `_suppress_presses`, because a click that retargets the window is asking for the window, not swinging. MEASURED: without the suppression the refocus click reached `PlayerCombat` as a light attack, and a COMMITTED attack owns the body - which is why the player could not walk afterwards. Any future "click to recover" path must suppress the press for the same reason.
- Do not gate the capture recovery on the focus belief. That belief can be wrong in either direction, and a click that reached this game is itself the proof that the player is here.

## Pause and the HUD (recorded 2026-09-14, Milestone 13)

- Pause is `get_tree().paused = true` and NOTHING else. Never fake a pause with `Engine.time_scale`: a zero scale with an UNPAUSED tree is exactly the externally-stopped-clock state the input layer already had to recover from (M10.6, M10.10), and it renders frames while making the game unplayable.
- ESCAPE PAUSES ON ITS FIRST PRESS - this AMENDS the earlier reading that Escape's only job is to own `mouse_look_enabled`. Escape still releases the cursor and still clears the capture intent; the pause panel now performs that release itself through `CascadiaInput.set_mouse_look(false)` instead of writing the flag, and marks the event handled so the input layer does not repeat the work. The superseded two-press layering (first press freed the cursor, second opened the panel) was measured as confusing and was replaced by the user's decision. Any future change here must keep the pause ESCAPABLE by the same key that opened it: a trap is worse than a two-press shortcut.
- The pause panel must be HIDDEN (`visible = false`, not merely transparent) while unpaused, and every decorative Control inside it must be `MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE`. Only Buttons keep STOP, and only while the panel is open. `focus_input_routing_probe_debug` enumerates every VISIBLE Control with a non-IGNORE filter that could take a click at the centre of the viewport, and light/heavy attacks are bound to MOUSE BUTTONS, so a decorative panel that consumes clicks silently eats attacks.
- `PauseMenu` owns NO gameplay state: one boolean derived from `get_tree().paused`, plus its own visibility. It calls the existing `GameStateSave` and displays that service's RETURNED `Result` code, so a refusal can never be presented as a success. Never reimplement save, load or run state in a UI.
- A UI LABEL IS NEVER AN AUTHORITY. The HUD stores only the text it displays and re-reads each owner. Godot runs `_ready()` in TREE ORDER, and in `main.tscn` the HUD is a sibling placed BEFORE `TestEnvironment`, so a display that only waits for signals shows the owners' pre-`_ready()` defaults indefinitely - measured on screen as `HEALTH 0 / 100` beside a combat overlay reading `100/100`. A display must refresh once when its owner connections land, and a probe must assert a displayed value AT REST, not only after a change: a change-only assertion passes happily while the at-rest value is wrong.

## Target lock-on (recorded 2026-09-14, Milestone 12)

- Lock-on is a TOGGLE on the EXISTING `lock_on` action (TAB, and joypad button 8 = R3). Do NOT rebind it and do NOT add a second toggle action. While a lock is held, `target_cycle_left` / `target_cycle_right` cycle through the valid targets - the Bloodborne/Souls convention the user chose.
- `target_cycle_left` / `target_cycle_right` are bound to joypad axis 2 at -1.0 / +1.0 plus mouse wheel. Axis 2 is ALSO `camera_look_left` / `camera_look_right`, so the same physical input would otherwise both turn the camera and cycle targets. R and F8/F9 are forbidden for new bindings: R is already bound twice (`critical` and `restart`) and the host owns F8/F9.
- That conflict is resolved the project's ESTABLISHED way - gameplay DECLARES an intent and the input layer honours it. `TargetingComponent` sets `CascadiaInput.look_yaw_suppressed` while a lock is held and `_update_look()` drops ONLY the horizontal stick component. Mouse motion and the vertical stick are untouched.
- The input layer must NEVER derive lock state per frame, and the camera must never become the lock authority. This is the same rule as the resume-only focus reconciliation: a per-frame reconciliation that can create its own state is a defect this project already fixed once.
- Target validity has exactly ONE owner: `CombatParticipant.is_usable_target()` / `target_refusal()`. Do not add a second validity authority.
- Auto-release is REQUIRED on all four causes, each counted separately: target defeated, target invalid or freed, target beyond `release_range`, and player death.
- The camera yaws to frame the locked target on the shortest arc and NEVER writes pitch; roll stays zero by construction. The rig only READS lock state.
- Lock-on-RELATIVE dodge direction is still NOT implemented. Dodge stays camera-relative; that is a separate deferred pass.
- `EnemyAttacker.target_group` is the ENEMY targeting the PLAYER - a different concern. Never conflate it with player lock-on.
